CLAIM UNDER TEST: "folder import can no longer overwrite an existing
destination file, and can no longer delete a source file whose import was
skipped." Attacked each listed vector plus independent empirical checks.

1. Fake-stub attack (does the shipped race test prove the REAL write path is
   exclusive?) — PARTIALLY CONFIRMED AS A DEFECT IN THE TEST, but the
   underlying PRODUCTION CODE is independently verified correct:
   - Read TestImportOneFile_ConcurrentCreateBetweenCheckAndWriteSkipsWithoutOverwrite
     body (handlers_test.go:2576-2612) line by line. deps.write is fully
     overridden; it does call the real store.CreateExclusive twice (so the
     storage-layer exclusivity mechanism itself is real), but this insulates
     the test from whether defaultImportDeps() is actually wired to
     CreateExclusive in production.
   - Verified by mutation on a scratch copy (not the repo): reverted
     `write: func(...) { return store.CreateExclusive(...) }` back to
     `store.WriteFile(...)` at handlers.go:565, keeping the ErrExist branch.
     Ran `go test -race -count=1 -v ./internal/handlers/explorer/...`: ALL
     ~100 tests pass, including the new race test itself. The shipped test
     suite would NOT catch a regression that silently reverts the fix's core
     wiring. (The primary checker's manifest independently found and
     disclosed this same gap as "advisory, not a criterion" — I confirmed it
     myself rather than taking their word for it.)
   - To determine whether the CURRENT, unmutated production code is actually
     correct (not just "looks correct on inspection"), I added a throwaway
     test file exercising real goroutines against UNMODIFIED
     defaultImportDeps() (no deps.write override at all): 16 concurrent
     importOneFile("racer2.csv", false, deps) calls at the same destination
     name. Ran `go test -race -count=10`: exactly 1 of 16 imported, 15
     skipped, 0 unexpected, every run, 10/10, -race clean. This is genuine
     evidence the production write path (store.CreateExclusive via hard-link,
     storage.go:365-410) is exclusive under real concurrency, not just when a
     stub is asked to behave that way. File was deleted immediately after;
     `git status --short internal/handlers/explorer/` confirms no residue.
   - Net: the claim holds functionally, but the delivered test suite has a
     real regression-protection gap (no test pins the defaultImportDeps()
     binding). Flagging as a finding, not failing the task, since (a) the
     literal R2.md acceptance bullet — "a test in which the destination is
     created between the check and the write ... exactly one write wins, the
     loser reports skipped, the destination holds the winner's bytes, and the
     loser's source file still exists on disk" — is met by the shipped test
     as written, and (b) I independently proved the actual shipped code
     satisfies the claim under real concurrency.

2. ErrExist branch never reaches source deletion — CONFIRMED, no defect.
   Traced every return in importOneFile (handlers.go:632-733): the ErrExist
   branch (695-697) and the step-3 Stat pre-check (679-681) both `return`
   immediately; deps.removeSrc is called at exactly one site (line 725),
   reached only after write success + readback length match + Status
   "imported". Both skip paths are also exercised by real tests
   (TestHandleImport_CollisionSkipsAndKeepsSource for the Stat path,
   TestImportOneFile_ConcurrentCreateBetweenCheckAndWriteSkipsWithoutOverwrite
   for the ErrExist path) asserting SourceDeleted==false and mustExist(src).

3. Divergent skip-reason strings — NOT FOUND. Step 3
   ("already exists in the data folder") and the ErrExist branch
   ("already exists in the data folder") are byte-identical
   (handlers.go:679-681 vs 695-697). Confirmed by direct read of both
   literals, not by trusting test names.

4. Non-ErrExist write error (permission/IO) with destination present —
   NOT FOUND. `if err := deps.write(...); err != nil { if errors.Is(err,
   os.ErrExist) {...} return importOutcome{..., "rejected", "could not save
   the file"} }` (handlers.go:694-699): any non-ErrExist error returns
   "rejected" immediately, before step 5/6, so source is never deleted on
   this path either. TestImportOneFile_WriteFailureNeverDeletes exercises
   this (os.ErrPermission stub) and asserts removeSrc unreached.

5. Encryption / readback length under an encrypted store — pre-existing
   behavior, not changed by R2 (CreateExclusive already existed and was
   already tested for the encrypted round-trip case in
   internal/services/storage/create_exclusive_test.go, which predates this
   diff and is outside R2's file scope). No regression introduced here; step
   5's readback+length check is byte-identical to HEAD (structural diff
   confirmed no delta in that block).

6. Concurrency mechanism — genuinely serializes at the OS level, not merely
   "relies on the filesystem" as a hand-wave: storage.go's createExclusive
   stages a temp file then calls os.Link(tmp, path), which is atomic and
   fails EEXIST for every loser; confirmed via storage's own
   TestCreateExclusiveLetsExactlyOneConcurrentWriterWin (real goroutines,
   -race) AND my own explorer-level real-goroutine test above.

